---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
// Every radio the flasher knows, written from its catalogue.
import RadioTable from '../components/RadioTable.astro';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';

/** The site's top, with a slash at the end. */
const top = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---

<Page
  title="Radios the flasher supports"
  description="Every radio the flasher can put OpenRTX on, how it reaches it, whether that was checked on a real radio, and what it can back up first."
  current="radios"
>
  <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none prose-a:text-primary">
    <p class="max-w-3xl">
      This table is written from the flasher's own list of radios, so it is always what the tool does today. For what
      OpenRTX itself can do on each radio once it is flashed, see the{' '}
      <a href="https://openrtx.org/#/dev_status">development status</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <RadioTable />
    </div>
    <h2>Reading the table</h2>
    <ul class="max-w-3xl">
      <li>
        <strong>Flashing checked on a real radio</strong> means somebody flashed that model from the web page and the
        radio started OpenRTX. The <a href="https://github.com/turnrye/openrtx-web-flasher/blob/main/docs/verification.md"
          >bench record</a
        > has each entry. Until a model is checked, the flasher asks before it writes to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Backup before flashing</strong> says whether the flasher can save everything the radio holds. Most radios
        have gaps: a part of their memory that cannot be read yet. Each gap is listed on{' '}
        <a href={`${top}backups/`}>what a backup keeps</a>, with what would be lost and whether it can be saved another
        way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Otherwise</strong> is the usual tool for that radio, for browsers that cannot talk to it and for getting
        a radio back when a flash went wrong.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="max-w-3xl">
      A radio that is not here may still run OpenRTX: the <a href="https://openrtx.org/#/hardware/hardware"
        >hardware pages</a
      > cover every port, and the <a href="https://openrtx.org/#/user_guide">users' guide</a> has the manual way to flash.
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>
